129	<body>
130	<main class="wrap">
131	  <p class="intro">Six other ways to show replacements for a locked span. Click inside a page, then use the keys listed under it. The options are canned; nothing here calls a model.</p>
132	
133	  <nav class="tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Interfaces">
134	    <button role="tab" aria-selected="true" data-for="drum">Drum</button>
135	    <button role="tab" aria-selected="false" data-for="margin">Margin</button>
136	    <button role="tab" aria-selected="false" data-for="inter">Interlinear</button>
137	    <button role="tab" aria-selected="false" data-for="map">Tone map</button>
138	    <button role="tab" aria-selected="false" data-for="tumblers">Tumblers</button>
139	    <button role="tab" aria-selected="false" data-for="sheet">Proof sheet</button>
140	  </nav>
141	
142	  <!-- 1. Drum -->
143	  <section class="concept" id="drum" role="tabpanel">
144	    <header class="c-head"><h2>Drum</h2><p>The locked word spins in place, with the previous and next options faded above and below it. There is no panel, so your eyes never leave the sentence.</p></header>
145	    <div class="stage" tabindex="0">
146	      <p class="prose faint">Morning came early to the valley, and the bells started before anyone was ready for them.</p>
147	      <p class="prose">It was a <span class="slot lock" id="drum-slot"><span class="ghost up"></span><span class="w"></span><span class="ghost down"></span></span> day for the whole village, and nobody wanted it to end.</p>
148	      <p class="prose faint">By noon the square was full, and the baker had run out of bread twice.</p>
149	      <p class="status" aria-live="polite"></p>
150	    </div>
151	    <p class="keys"><kbd>↑</kbd> <kbd>↓</kbd> or scroll over the word · click a faded option · <kbd>Enter</kbd> keep · <kbd>Esc</kbd> original</p>
152	    <dl class="trade"><div><dt>Good for</dt><dd>Single words and fast flicking. On touch it becomes a vertical swipe on the word.</dd></div><div><dt>Costs</dt><dd>You see three options at a time, so it suits flicking more than browsing. It covers a sliver of the lines above and below.</dd></div></dl>
153	  </section>
154	
155	  <!-- 2. Margin -->
156	  <section class="concept" id="margin" role="tabpanel" hidden>
157	    <header class="c-head"><h2>Margin</h2><p>Options sit in the page margin as marginal notes, tied to the locked words by a leader line. Nothing covers the writing, and a steer box takes a direction in your own words.</p></header>
158	    <div class="stage" tabindex="0">
159	      <svg class="thread" aria-hidden="true"></svg>
160	      <div class="rail-grid">
161	        <div>
162	          <p class="prose faint">It was a happy day for the whole village, and nobody wanted it to end.</p>
163	          <p class="prose"><span id="rail-span" class="lock"></span>, its shutters closed against the wind. Nobody had lived there since the flood.</p>
164	        </div>
165	        <aside class="rail" aria-label="Alternatives">
166	          <div class="chips" role="group" aria-label="Kinds"></div>
167	          <ol></ol>
168	          <form class="steer"><input type="text" placeholder="make it… lonelier" aria-label="Steer the wordings"></form>
169	        </aside>
170	      </div>
171	      <p class="status" aria-live="polite"></p>
172	    </div>
173	    <p class="keys"><kbd>↑</kbd> <kbd>↓</kbd> preview · <kbd>Enter</kbd> use · <kbd>Esc</kbd> original · type a direction, then <kbd>Enter</kbd> (try “lonelier”, “ominous”, “warmer”)</p>
174	    <dl class="trade"><div><dt>Good for</dt><dd>Phrases, sentences and paragraphs. It matches the margin notes the room already writes.</dd></div><div><dt>Costs</dt><dd>It needs margin width. On a phone it drops below the paragraph.</dd></div></dl>
175	  </section>
176	
177	  <!-- 3. Interlinear -->
178	  <section class="concept" id="inter" role="tabpanel" hidden>
179	    <header class="c-head"><h2>Interlinear</h2><p>Four options are set as small lines directly under the sentence, like a gloss, with the changed words underlined. Press a number to use one.</p></header>
180	    <div class="stage" tabindex="0">
181	      <p class="prose faint">It was a happy day for the whole village, and nobody wanted it to end.</p>
182	      <p class="prose"><span id="int-span" class="lock"></span>, its shutters closed against the wind.</p>
183	      <ol class="gloss" aria-label="Alternatives"></ol>
184	      <p class="prose faint">Nobody had lived there since the flood, and the garden had gone back to bramble.</p>
185	      <p class="status" aria-live="polite"></p>
186	    </div>
187	    <p class="keys"><kbd>1</kbd>–<kbd>4</kbd> use · <kbd>↑</kbd> <kbd>↓</kbd> preview · <kbd>M</kbd> next four · <kbd>Esc</kbd> original · click the sentence to reopen</p>
188	    <dl class="trade"><div><dt>Good for</dt><dd>Comparing sentence rewrites line against line, right where they will go.</dd></div><div><dt>Costs</dt><dd>It pushes the next paragraph down while open, and shows four at a time.</dd></div></dl>
189	  </section>
190	
191	  <!-- 4. Tone map -->
192	  <section class="concept" id="map" role="tabpanel" hidden>
193	    <header class="c-head"><h2>Tone map</h2><p>Words are placed on a map: up is stronger, down crosses into opposites, left is casual and right is formal. Drag the dot and the nearest word drops into the sentence.</p></header>
194	    <div class="stage" tabindex="0">
195	      <p class="prose">It was a <span id="map-slot" class="lock">happy</span> day for the whole village, and nobody wanted it to end.</p>
196	      <div class="map" aria-label="Words by strength and register">
197	        <div class="ax x"></div><div class="ax y"></div>
198	        <span class="lbl" style="left:calc(50% + 6px);top:5px">stronger</span>
199	        <span class="lbl" style="left:6px;top:calc(50% - 16px)">neutral</span>
200	        <span class="lbl" style="left:calc(50% + 6px);bottom:5px">opposite</span>
201	        <span class="lbl" style="left:6px;bottom:5px">← casual</span>
202	        <span class="lbl" style="right:6px;bottom:5px">formal →</span>
203	        <div class="dot"></div>
204	      </div>
205	      <p class="status" aria-live="polite"></p>
206	    </div>
207	    <p class="keys">drag or click the map · arrow keys move the dot · <kbd>Enter</kbd> keep · <kbd>Esc</kbd> original</p>
208	    <dl class="trade"><div><dt>Good for</dt><dd>The “thesaurus, but wider” case: finding the word one notch stronger, plainer or more formal.</dd></div><div><dt>Costs</dt><dd>Words only. Placing each word takes two Score judgements (strength and register) on top of the word list.</dd></div></dl>
209	  </section>
210	
211	  <!-- 5. Tumblers -->
212	  <section class="concept" id="tumblers" role="tabpanel" hidden>
213	    <header class="c-head"><h2>Tumblers</h2><p>Lock a sentence and every swappable word becomes its own small drum. Recompose it word by word, or take a whole-sentence rewrite below.</p></header>
214	    <div class="stage" tabindex="0">
215	      <p class="prose faint">It was a happy day for the whole village, and nobody wanted it to end.</p>
216	      <p class="prose tum-line" id="tum-line"></p>
217	      <div class="whole"><p>Or the whole sentence:</p></div>
218	      <p class="status" aria-live="polite"></p>
219	    </div>
220	    <p class="keys"><kbd>←</kbd> <kbd>→</kbd> choose a word · <kbd>↑</kbd> <kbd>↓</kbd> or scroll to turn it · <kbd>Enter</kbd> keep · <kbd>Esc</kbd> reset</p>
221	    <dl class="trade"><div><dt>Good for</dt><dd>Reworking one sentence while keeping most of it.</dd></div><div><dt>Costs</dt><dd>Swaps can break agreement (tense, a/an, plurals), so the app would need a quick model check on the finished sentence.</dd></div></dl>
222	  </section>
223	
224	  <!-- 6. Proof sheet -->
225	  <section class="concept" id="sheet" role="tabpanel" hidden>
226	    <header class="c-head"><h2>Proof sheet</h2><p>The passage is set several times side by side, each with a different option in place, so you judge the rhythm in context instead of in a list.</p></header>
227	    <div class="stage" tabindex="0">
228	      <div class="sheet"></div>
229	      <div class="sheet-foot"><button type="button">Next three</button></div>
230	      <p class="status" aria-live="polite"></p>
231	    </div>
232	    <p class="keys">arrow keys move · <kbd>1</kbd>–<kbd>4</kbd> or <kbd>Enter</kbd> use · <kbd>N</kbd> next three · <kbd>Esc</kbd> original</p>
233	    <dl class="trade"><div><dt>Good for</dt><dd>Sentences where the sound in context decides.</dd></div><div><dt>Costs</dt><dd>It covers the manuscript while open and shows three options at a time.</dd></div></dl>
234	  </section>
235	
236	  <section class="compare">
237	    <h3>How they compare</h3>
238	    <table>
239	      <thead><tr><th>Interface</th><th>The writing stays readable</th><th>Best at</th><th class="opt">What it adds to today’s code</th></tr></thead>
240	      <tbody>
241	        <tr><td>Drum</td><td>Covers a sliver above and below</td><td>Single words</td><td class="opt">Same lock and preview; an overlay replaces the list</td></tr>
242	        <tr><td>Margin</td><td>Fully</td><td>Phrases to paragraphs, steering</td><td class="opt">Same lock and preview; a margin column and a steer field</td></tr>
243	        <tr><td>Interlinear</td><td>Fully; pushes text down</td><td>Sentences</td><td class="opt">Same lock; options become a widget in the page</td></tr>
244	        <tr><td>Tone map</td><td>Panel over the page</td><td>Word nuance</td><td class="opt">A second view; two Score judgements per word</td></tr>
245	        <tr><td>Tumblers</td><td>Fully</td><td>Reworking one sentence</td><td class="opt">Per-word options and a final grammar check</td></tr>
246	        <tr><td>Proof sheet</td><td>Panel over the page</td><td>Rhythm in context</td><td class="opt">A grid view of the same options</td></tr>
247	        <tr class="now"><td>Now: list</td><td>Covers the lines below</td><td>Browsing many options</td><td class="opt">Built</td></tr>
248	      </tbody>
249	    </table>
250	    <p class="rec"><strong>My pick:</strong> switch by selection size, the way the lenses already do. Use the Drum for a single word and the Margin for anything longer. The Tone map fits as a second view inside word mode, one key away. Tumblers is the most distinctive, but it needs a grammar check across swaps, so I would build it after the others.</p>
251	  </section>
252	</main>
